        "The 32% opposition in a district this wealthy is the most analytically striking competitive signal in the sequence. Denise Crosswhite Hader won 68%, but the 32% who chose an alternative in a $107K district are not lower-income protest voters — they are organized professional households with specific governance concerns. The 5.10% Asian and 6.62% Black professional communities contribute to this opposition; in an affluent suburb, minority professional households often have higher governance expectations and are more responsive to candidates who address school quality, development, and community investment with specificity. A 32% opposition coalition in a district with this income and education profile has the money, the networks, and the motivation to become decisive in a cycle where the incumbent's governance record becomes the central issue."
