        "HD-64 is Lawton's urban core — the most economically challenged, most diverse, and most politically competitive district in this ten-seat sequence. At $47,406 median income (the lowest in the group) and 55.7% renter rate (the highest), this is a constituency of working families and lower-income households who are not sharing equally in the economic base that Fort Sill and the broader Lawton economy provide. The demographic profile — less than 50% non-Hispanic white, with 18% Black, 15% Hispanic, 3% Asian, and an estimated 14% AIAN gap — reflects both the military diversity of the Fort Sill community and Lawton's established minority communities. The 54-46 partisan result is the most competitive in southwestern Oklahoma and reflects a constituency that votes on material concerns: economic security, housing, healthcare, and whether government is actually working for them.",
