        { name: "Conflating the Hispanic and Black Communities Into a Single Minority Agenda", tag: "avoid", why: "HD-78's 21% Hispanic and 11% Black communities have overlapping but distinct legislative needs, and conflating them into a homogeneous minority constituency misses both. The Hispanic community's largest specific concerns involve workforce access, immigration-related economic insecurity, and language-barrier service delivery. The Black community's specific concerns include historical economic disparity, educational access, and public safety equity. Serving both well means addressing each with the specificity their individual histories and circumstances demand — a representative who merges them into a single 'diversity' priority is acknowledging the demographics without serving the constituents." },
        { name: "Assuming a 31.5% College Rate Means an Inattentive Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "At 31.5% college attainment — substantially higher than HD-77's 13.7% — this district contains a meaningfully educated professional class that is both engaged and discerning. Educated urban Democrats in an unopposed district are not passive: they track legislative records, evaluate substantive policy positions, and are capable of organizing a primary challenge when they conclude their representative has become complacent. An unopposed result in a district this educated is not a mandate — it is the absence of a viable challenger this cycle. The representative who treats it as permission for minimal engagement will eventually discover what it costs." }
