        "The Hispanic community at 25% is the district's most politically significant demographic variable: at this size, in an OKC metro context with a young median age and high renter rate, the Latino community has the economic concentration and community infrastructure to organize politically when motivated. The specific concerns of OKC's Hispanic working-class families — housing stability, workforce access, language-accessible services — are not being fully addressed if 42% of the district is voting for an alternative. A candidate who engages the Hispanic community with measurable policy commitments, and who simultaneously addresses the housing and economic security concerns of the district's Black, white, and AIAN renters, is building the cross-community coalition that makes 58% into a genuine majority rather than a temporary margin held by low minority turnout."
