        "Ellyn Hefner's 62-38 result reflects the Democratic coalition advantage in a majority-renter, diverse urban district — but the 38% Republican opposition is a real constituency whose character matters. In a district where white residents at 43.35% could anchor a Republican-leaning coalition if economic frustration deepens, the Democratic margin depends on genuine delivery for Hispanic and Black constituencies who are not locked in by party loyalty in the same way older white progressive voters might be. The 12.80% Black community represents a constituency that can shift elections through turnout if their specific concerns — economic mobility, neighborhood investment, educational access — are addressed with substantive attention rather than symbolic acknowledgment. A candidate who treats HD-87 as a diverse district where all communities automatically lean Democratic is misreading the coalition arithmetic; the 62% requires active, specific work in each of the district's major communities simultaneously."
