      issues: [
        { name: "Cross-Cultural Working-Class Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "With White (47.22%), Hispanic (25.56%), Black (11.84%), and Asian (5.11%) populations — plus the ~10.27% AIAN gap — HD-90 is a genuinely multi-ethnic district where the working-class economic pressures shared across communities are the strongest organizing principle. At $59,900 income, 32.8 median age, and 41.7% renter rate, housing affordability, job quality, and healthcare access are the material concerns that unite this diverse constituency. A representative who builds a governing agenda around these shared economic security priorities rather than identity-specific appeals can build the broadest available coalition in a district where no single group constitutes a majority." },
        { name: "Youth and Early-Career Economic Investment", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 32.8-year median age and 41.7% renter rate signal a constituency dominated by young adults in the early stages of career and family formation across multiple ethnic backgrounds, all facing similar pressures around housing access, childcare costs, and employment stability. In a diverse district this young, the representative who speaks directly to the economic constraints of early-career households — housing, childcare, job quality — speaks to the dominant life-stage experience of the plurality of constituents. These investments build the broadest cross-demographic coalition available, because they address what most residents are actually navigating regardless of ethnicity." },
        { name: "The 34% Opposition Is the Majority Population", tag: "careful", why: "In a district where white residents constitute 47.22% but non-white residents collectively constitute roughly 52.78% of reported demographics (plus the ~10% AIAN gap), the 34% Democratic opposition almost certainly reflects low minority voter turnout rather than minority community political preferences. The 66% Republican margin rests on a participation differential that is structurally fragile if Hispanic, Black, and Asian civic engagement increases. Any holder of this seat who does not actively cultivate cross-community constituent relationships is relying on the turnout differential to hold indefinitely — a brittle foundation for a majority-minority district." },
        { name: "Governing for the White Plurality While the Minority Majority Is Underserved", tag: "avoid", why: "With non-white residents constituting roughly half or more of the population by any accounting, a representative who governs primarily for the 47.22% white plurality is governing for a plurality that may not even be a numerical majority. The Hispanic, Black, and Asian communities have constituency needs that require specific policy attention and constituent service, and their continued low turnout is not a permanent structural feature of the district — it is a product of disengagement that can shift when a candidate or issue motivates these communities to participate at rates that match their population share." },
        { name: "Assuming the 66% Republican Result Reflects the District's Political Identity", tag: "avoid", why: "A majority-minority district by population that votes 66% Republican is a demographic paradox created by differential turnout, not by the political preferences of the full constituency. Candidates of either party who treat this result as a reliable indicator of durable Republican dominance are misreading the structural reality of a district where a majority of residents belong to communities that have not been fully mobilized electorally. The 66% is one effective organizing cycle away from a competitive seat for any candidate who invests seriously in multi-ethnic community engagement." }
      ],
      memoHeadline: "OKC's Demographic Paradox: A Majority-Minority Population Voting 66% Republican, Built on Differential Turnout That Won't Hold Indefinitely",
      memoParagraphs: [
        "HD-90 presents the most analytically striking paradox in this nine-district sequence: a district whose population is majority non-white by any reasonable accounting — White (47.22%), Hispanic (25.56%), Black (11.84%), Asian (5.11%), and an approximately 10.27% AIAN gap, placing non-white residents at roughly 52-63% of the population — yet produces a 66-34 Republican result. At $59,900 median income, 32.8-year median age, and 41.7% renter rate, this is not a wealthy, established homeowner community that naturally produces large Republican margins. It is a young, diverse, majority-renter district where economic pressures around housing affordability, job quality, and early-career financial stability are the dominant concerns. Emily Gise's 66-34 result is almost certainly a product of differential voter turnout — with the white plurality voting at substantially higher rates than Hispanic, Black, and Asian communities — rather than a reflection of minority community political preferences.",
        "The strategic implications of this differential are the defining fact of HD-90's competitive landscape. Any shift in minority civic engagement — increased Hispanic and Black voter registration, targeted turnout investment by community organizations, or a mobilizing issue that activates communities currently underrepresented in the electorate — would fundamentally alter the seat's partisan math. The Republican holder of this seat faces a structural vulnerability that a dominant margin can obscure: the demographic majority of the district does not appear in the electoral result. Meanwhile, any Democratic challenger who invests in genuine, sustained minority community organizing — specific economic-issue-based engagement with Hispanic, Black, and Asian households — is building toward the electoral math that already exists in the population and simply has not been activated. A cross-cultural economic security message around housing, jobs, and healthcare is the unifying agenda for a constituency this diverse at this income level."
      ],
      memoBullets: [
        "Recognize the demographic paradox at the center of HD-90's politics: a majority-minority population producing a 66% Republican result reflects differential turnout, not minority community alignment — the representative who understands this governs and campaigns differently than one who takes 66% as a structural floor",
        "Build around cross-cultural economic security: housing affordability, job quality, and early-career financial stability are the material concerns shared by the district's White, Hispanic, Black, and Asian constituents at 32.8-year median age and $59,900 income — this is the unifying agenda that crosses demographic lines",
        "Invest specific constituent service in Hispanic (25.56%) and Black (11.84%) communities whose lower relative turnout currently sustains the Republican majority; these communities' economic and housing concerns are real and persistent, and the representative who serves them builds a durable relationship that the electoral result does not yet reflect",
        "Do not treat 66% as structural — a majority-minority district voting Republican based on differential turnout is one effective organizing cycle away from a competitive seat; the incumbent's defense is to earn multi-community engagement across demographic lines, not to assume the differential will persist indefinitely"
      ]
