        { name: "Assuming the 100% Republican Designation Reflects a Republican-Leaning Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "The uncontested 2024 result that produced a '100% Republican' placeholder for a now-vacant seat does not describe the political preferences of a near-majority Hispanic, majority-renter, low-income district. A constituency that is 49.99% Hispanic, 61.8% renter, at $44,265 income with a 31.4 median age is not natural Republican territory by any demographic logic — it is an unorganized competitive opportunity, and the first candidate who takes it seriously will find that the partisan placeholder was an artifact of non-competition, not political preference." },
        { name: "A Campaign That Only Speaks to the Hispanic Near-Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "Despite the near-majority Hispanic population, winning HD-92 durably requires a multi-community coalition message that addresses housing affordability and economic security across the 30.82% white, 9.24% Black, and 7.58% AIAN communities as well. A campaign designed exclusively for the Hispanic plurality will win less convincingly than one that simultaneously addresses the shared economic concerns of the district's multi-ethnic working-class majority. The coalition math in HD-92 requires a message that Hispanic, Black, and white working-class residents can each hear as speaking to their specific circumstances." }
