        { name: "Black Community Economic and Material Priorities", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 49.90% Black — effectively a majority-Black district — the African American community's economic mobility, public safety, and healthcare access concerns are the core governance mandate for any representative in HD-97. At $53,521 median income with 42.0% renters, economic precarity is a daily material reality for a substantial portion of this community, and the gap between good representation and adequate representation translates directly into household outcomes for families navigating housing insecurity, healthcare costs, and constrained economic mobility in northeast OKC. A representative who delivers specific, concrete results — healthcare access expanded, economic development secured, housing stability improved — is building the durable relationship that genuine service creates, distinct from the margin that an unopposed electoral result simply provides." },
        { name: "Constituent Service as Governance Standard Without Electoral Accountability", tag: "lean-into", why: "Unopposed status removes the primary market mechanism that competitive elections impose — the incentive gradient that drives constituent engagement, policy responsiveness, and community presence. In a district with $53,521 income, 42% renters, and significant economic pressure on the Black community, the needs are specific, material, and ongoing regardless of who appears on the ballot. The governance risk is not electoral defeat but the gradual erosion of the representation standard: when there is no electoral consequence for minimal service delivery, the representative must impose their own accountability standard rather than rely on external pressure. The constituent who cannot access their representative, whose call goes unreturned, whose specific community need goes unaddressed, experiences the gap between an unopposed result and genuine representation in real terms." },
        { name: "Hispanic Community Representation at 8.32%", tag: "careful", why: "The 8.32% Hispanic community in a majority-Black northeast OKC district can become invisible when the dominant demographic narrative focuses on the Black community's plurality status. The Hispanic community has distinct concerns — workforce participation, housing affordability, language-access services, and the experience of being a minority within a majority-minority district — that exist alongside the Black community's priorities but are not identical to them. A representative who explicitly includes the Hispanic constituency in constituent outreach, community engagement, and legislative priority-setting is governing the full district rather than the numerically dominant group, and is building the broader coalition relationships that sustain representation across demographic changes." },
        { name: "Treating Unopposed Status as License to Neglect Constituent Service", tag: "avoid", why: "The absence of electoral competition does not eliminate constituency need; it eliminates the feedback mechanism that competition provides. In a district with 42% renters and $53,521 income, the economic pressures on constituents are real and ongoing regardless of who is on the ballot — and the most vulnerable households, those facing housing insecurity and healthcare gaps, have the fewest resources to advocate for themselves through channels other than their representative's office. A representative who interprets unopposed status as political security rather than as a responsibility to deliver at the highest standard is accepting the minimum representation standard precisely where the maximum is most needed." },
        { name: "Governing Through Symbolic Representation Without Material Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "This is a constituency with specific, pressing material needs in northeast OKC: healthcare access, housing stability, economic development, quality public services. A representative whose public profile is built primarily on identity representation and progressive positioning — rather than on the concrete constituent results, the legislation that changes daily conditions, the development dollars attracted, the community relationships that solve specific problems — is not fulfilling the mandate of a majority-Black, high-renter, working-class district whose residents need their representative to be effective, not merely present. In a district this economically constrained, the gap between symbolic representation and material delivery is measured in quality-of-life outcomes." }
