        { name: "Hispanic Community Engagement at 13.21%", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 13.21% Hispanic — one in seven residents — HD-98 has one of the more significant Hispanic populations in the OKC metro portion of this sequence, and this community's presence in a 67-33 Republican district is a governance reality that the partisan result substantially underrepresents. The Hispanic community in suburban OKC at this income level includes both working-class households in service and construction employment and professionally employed families who chose this suburb for school quality and community stability — groups with overlapping but distinct concerns around workforce conditions, educational access, and the sense of genuine inclusion in a majority-Anglo civic community. A representative who engages this constituency specifically, rather than acknowledging it symbolically, is recognizing that one-in-seven of the district's residents has a legitimate and specific claim on their state representative's attention." },
        { name: "AIAN Community at ~14.4% Gap", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported demographic categories account for only 85.62% of HD-98's population — a ~14.4% gap that is one of the largest in this eight-district sequence. In the OKC metro suburban context, this gap represents citizens of multiple tribal nations — potentially including Cheyenne and Arapaho, Kiowa, or other Oklahoma tribal nations whose members have established residence in suburban OKC communities. These constituents' governance interests in tribal-state economic development, Indian Health Service access, and the legislative relationships that determine whether tribal institutions can function effectively are real and ongoing regardless of how visible they are in the suburban Republican political narrative. A representative who engages this community is governing a more complete picture of the district's 38,000 residents." },
        { name: "Treating the 33% Opposition as a Rounding Error", tag: "avoid", why: "At 67-33, the opposition appears negligible — but in a district with 13% Hispanic, ~14% AIAN, and 5% Black communities comprising nearly one-third of residents, the 33% is not a rounding error. It is a substantial minority community constituency plus economically evaluated suburban homeowners who found the incumbent insufficient on specific grounds. Understanding where the 33% is concentrated and what drives it — whether in specific geographic precincts, specific minority community turnout patterns, or specific policy frustrations among white suburban voters — is the strategic work that determines whether a motivated challenger ever makes HD-98 competitive, and whether the 33% floor becomes a 40% ceiling with organized effort." },
        { name: "Passive Representation of Minority Communities Behind a Safe-Looking Margin", tag: "avoid", why: "With 13% Hispanic, ~14% AIAN, and 5% Black communities comprising roughly one-third of the district's residents, a representative who governs primarily for the Anglo homeowner majority is governing an abstraction of the district rather than the actual community of 38,000 people. These constituencies have specific economic and civic concerns — workforce conditions, tribal institutional relationships, economic mobility — that persist regardless of how they appear in the partisan result, and the organizational vacuum that neglect creates is the foundation on which competitive challengers build. A 67-33 margin that reflects differential turnout rather than genuine cross-community governance is a margin that a sufficiently motivated opposition can narrow." }
