        { name: "Black Community Economic Priorities at 44.55%", tag: "lean-into", why: "The near-majority Black population at 44.55% in an urban OKC context signals a community with specific economic mobility, public safety, healthcare, and educational access concerns that are the core governance priorities for this district's defining constituency. At $46,490 income and 64.2% renter rate, the economic precarity facing Black families in HD-99 is acute — and the 54% Democratic coalition holds together because candidates who address this community's material needs with specificity are performing the basic function of representation rather than its symbolic substitute. A legislative record that delivers specific, concrete improvements to the material conditions of Black households in this district — reduced housing cost burden, expanded healthcare access, improved public safety outcomes — is the foundation for a durable coalition rather than a cycle-to-cycle performance." },
        { name: "The 46% Opposition in a Majority-Minority Competitive District", tag: "careful", why: "A 54-46 margin in a majority-minority OKC urban district means the coalition requires active maintenance and cannot be taken for granted. The Black community's 44.55% is a floor, not a ceiling, for coalition-building — sustaining 54% requires either near-universal Black turnout at high rates or genuine cross-community coalition-building with the 13.40% Hispanic constituency and white voters whose 31% share includes both committed partisans and economic evaluators. The Hispanic community in this urban OKC context is not reliably Democratic by default; their political preferences are a function of candidate quality, economic conditions, and whether they perceive the representative as genuinely responsive to their specific concerns. At 54-46, every community's participation is a swing variable." },
        { name: "Treating 54% as a Safe Democratic Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "Nothing about 54-46 in a district with $46,490 income, 64.2% renters, and majority-minority demographics is structurally safe. Low-income, renter-majority, majority-minority constituencies are historically the most susceptible to turnout collapse when economic conditions worsen or when constituents perceive that their representative is not delivering on the material concerns that motivated their participation. A candidate who campaigns this district as a Democratic lock — relying on partisan geography rather than constituent service — is building a model of HD-99 that does not account for the economic fragility of the coalition and the ease with which turnout collapse translates to electoral defeat in a seat this competitive." },
        { name: "Assuming Coalition Cohesion Persists Without Material Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "Young, low-income, renter-majority constituencies are the most volatile electoral coalitions — enthusiasm and turnout can swing dramatically based on perceived performance rather than partisan identity. A representative who wins on the strength of community organizing and coalition-building but fails to translate that electoral success into tangible policy results — reduced housing cost burden, expanded healthcare access, improved economic mobility conditions — will find that the 46% opposition has a lower organizational bar to cross than the 54% result implies. In HD-99, the coalition was assembled around material promises; it is sustained only by material delivery, and any representative who loses sight of that equation will discover the 8-point margin disappears faster than it was built." }
