        "The 64.2% renter rate is the most consequential single data point in the district: it means housing affordability, tenant protections, housing quality standards, and eviction policy are not among many issues but the primary economic experience of the majority of constituent households. At $46,490 income, the gap between a manageable rent burden and economic crisis is narrow and real — and any legislative record that delivers tangible improvement on housing costs and tenant stability will translate into quality-of-life change for the majority of the district's residents in a way that few other policy areas can achieve. The 29.8% college attainment — higher than several wealthier suburban districts in this sequence — suggests a constituency with political awareness and organizational knowledge that can mobilize effectively around material concerns, and disengage with equal speed when those concerns are perceived as unaddressed."
