        { name: "Base-Only Mobilization in a District That Requires Persuasion", tag: "avoid", why: "In a 51-49 district, there is no base that is sufficient to win without cross-community persuasion. A Republican strategy that consolidates white homeowners without speaking to the 17% Black and 15% Hispanic communities' specific concerns will consistently underperform against a candidate who makes genuine coalition claims across racial lines. A Democratic strategy that mobilizes Black voters without addressing the economic concerns of Hispanic and white swing voters will find the same ceiling. HD-100 is won in the contested middle — the evaluative Hispanic voter, the economic-issue-driven white voter who doesn't vote on party identity, the Black voter whose enthusiasm and turnout depend on perceived responsiveness — not at the partisan edges." },
        { name: "Assuming Partisan Geography Explains the 51% Result", tag: "avoid", why: "A 51-49 result in a genuinely diverse, educated suburban district is not evidence of structural Republican advantage — it is evidence that Marilyn Stark outperformed by one percentage point in a specific 2024 contest. The demographic profile of HD-100 — diverse, educated, with large and politically engaged Black and Hispanic communities — does not favor a structural Republican majority; it favors competitive elections determined by candidate quality and coalition management. Any candidate who enters 2026 treating this seat as safely held or safely attainable without a genuine multi-community campaign strategy will discover that 51-49 was not an aberration but a baseline, and that the resources and relationships built between elections determine who holds the advantage when the cycle turns." }
