        "HD-101 is a prosperous Oklahoma City metro suburb — $80,730 median income, 21.2% renter rate (implying roughly 78.8% homeownership), and 39.1 median age — whose established homeowner community has invested substantially in the neighborhood stability and school quality that define the district's character. Robert Manger's 52-48 Republican result is the second-most-competitive in this eight-district sequence and the most competitive among Republican-held affluent suburban districts — a 4-point margin in a constituency that, by its demographic profile, might be expected to produce a more comfortable Republican advantage. The gap between that expectation and the actual 52-48 result is the central analytical question about HD-101: what is generating 48% opposition in a white-majority, affluent, homeowner-dominant OKC suburb where the demographic baseline suggests stronger partisan alignment?",
